  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 318 AM MDT Tue Jul 7 2026/ \n\nAn upper level shortwave moving through the area today brought a \nfew showers and thunderstorms to the area overnight. While these \nshowers and storms should dissipate and/or move eastward by \nsunrise, lingering instability and moisture this afternoon should \nallow for some storms to redevelop in the afternoon. While I can\u2019t\nrule out one or two stronger storms, widespread severe storms are\nnot expected.\n\nOur next weather maker will swing through the area on Wednesday \nas an upper level shortwave swings through Alberta. While the risk\nof an MCS-type setup appear to have diminished, this trough will \nstill have plenty of instability to work with, along with some \nmodest shear. With shear helping to organize the storms a bit, and\na very deep mixed layer, I can\u2019t rule out some of the high based \nstorms producing some larger hail and strong wind gusts, \nparticularly along the plains areas east of Interstate 15. The \nStorm Prediction Center has placed us in a marginal risk for \nsevere storms as well, so there is some agreement on the potential\nfor stronger storms. \n\nBeyond Wednesday, upper level ridging will quickly build and\nstrengthen over the SW CONUS, which will result in a quite rapid\nincrease temperatures across the region. Most areas will see\ntemperatures soar into the mid to upper 90s over the weekend, with\na few 100s possible as well depending on cloud cover. Temperatures\nwill lower slightly early next week as a trough swings by to the\nnorth, but warm temperatures are likely to linger through most of\nnext week as the ridge remains anchored to our south. Ludwig",
